.ids/ — the id allocation pool. One empty file per allocated id.

`spikes/harness/allocid.sh` allocates with `set -C; : > .ids/<id>`, which is
create-or-fail in one syscall, so a file here means the id is TAKEN and can
never be handed out again. The files are empty on purpose and MUST STAY EMPTY:
`allocid.sh:118` re-seeds from the tree on every invocation with
`: > "$IDS/$i"`, which truncates. Anything you write into an id file is erased
by the next allocation.

DO NOT DELETE THE FILES THAT HAVE NO QUEUE ROW. That is the whole reason this
README exists. (H64, ATOM-3, 2026-08-17.)

    H91  H99  S96  S97  S98  S99

These six are RESERVED FIXTURES, not allocations. They are the synthetic ids
inside the selfcheck strings of `journalcheck.py`, `idscope.py` and
`refcheck.py` — chosen high so a real allocation would not reach them. Nothing
enforced that: `allocid.sh` deliberately does not scan code (H57's attack
measured why — scanning code reserves `Q2`, `Q3`, `B6`, `B16`, which are
variable names and grep flags), so the allocator cannot see a fixture. When real
allocation reaches H91, `refcheck` check 5 and `idscope` read a selfcheck string
as a queue row.

They are reserved here today only by ACCIDENT: `WORK_QUEUE.md`'s H64 row names
them in prose, the seed reads every tracked `*.md`, and so the row documenting
the hazard reserved the ids it warns about. Measured 2026-08-17: highest real
`CLAIM` was H68 while `.ids/H91` and `.ids/H99` already existed.

An accident is not a mechanism. These six files are the only ids in the pool
with no queue row and no claim, which makes them exactly what a tidy-up would
delete first — and deleting them silently restores the collision with no error
anywhere. `spikes/harness/test_h64_id_reservations.sh` fails if one goes
missing, becomes untracked, or stops being reachable by the allocator.

To retire a reservation: delete the fixture id from the module that uses it
FIRST, then remove the file. Never the other way round.
